meta {
  name: 06 Center dashboard while blocked
  type: http
  seq: 7
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/centers/me/students
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

docs {
  The half of the design that is easy to break later, so it is worth checking
  by hand at least once.

  With the center BLOCKED, every one of these must still answer 200:

  | Request | |
  |---|---|
  | `GET /api/centers/me/students`         | this one |
  | `GET /api/centers/me/students/{id}`    | Center Students / 03 |
  | `PATCH /api/centers/me/students/{id}`  | Center Students / 04 |
  | `DELETE /api/centers/me/students/{id}` | Center Students / 06 |
  | `GET /api/centers/me`                  | Center Profile / 01 |
  | `PATCH /api/centers/me`                | Center Profile / 02 |
  | `GET /api/centers/me/subscription`     | Center Subscription / 01 |
  | `GET /api/centers/me/usage`            | Center Subscription / 02 |

  Only `POST /students` and `POST /students/{id}/activation-key` refuse.

  ## Why so permissive

  The one thing a lapsed center most needs to do is pay. Locking it out of the
  dashboard makes that less likely, not more — the subscription page is
  literally the page that explains the block and leads to the fix, and the
  profile holds the billing contact details someone may need to correct first.

  Removal stays open for the same reason: refusing it would trap a center above
  the seat count it is trying to get back down to before paying.

  The pressure belongs on creating NEW access, not on the door to paying.

  ## If one of these ever returns 403

  Someone has moved CenterSubscriptionGuard to the class level. That change
  looks like hardening and is a revenue bug. `test/center-blocked-surface.spec
  .ts` pins all eleven routes so it fails in CI first, but this request is how
  you would notice by hand.
}
